<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stalinism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stalinism is the system of rule and the policies associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from the late 1920s until 1953. It combined a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> and a personality cult with forced collectivisation of agriculture, rapid industrialisation through central planning, and mass terror by the secret police, including show trials and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps. The term is also used for regimes elsewhere, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>, that copied the model.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stalinism.jpg" alt="Stalinism" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Stalin_in_1937_with_his_pipe_smoking.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Stalinism developed from Leninism. Its central doctrine was socialism in one country, which Stalin developed from December 1924 and elaborated in his writings of 1925–26, in the factional struggle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a>. Other Bolshevik leaders had seen the revolution of 1917 as the beginning of a world revolution, but it had not spread, and Stalin argued that the socialist state must grow stronger before it could wither away, since counter-revolutionary elements would try to derail the transition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>.</p><p>The word came into use in the mid-1930s, when Lazar Kaganovich reportedly proposed replacing Long Live Leninism with Long Live Stalinism. Stalin dismissed this as excessive and as feeding a cult of personality that might later be turned against him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>At the start of the 1930s Stalin launched what became known as the Great Turn, abandoning the mixed economy of the New Economic Policy for a planned economy, with rapid industrialisation and mass collectivisation of agriculture. A cult of Stalin spread from 1929 to 1953 in architecture, statues and posters, in which he was cast as father, saviour and warrior.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Terror</a>, from September 1936 to December 1938, was the peak of political repression, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a>, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a>. Hundreds of thousands of party officials, military officers, intellectuals and ordinary citizens were sentenced and either executed or sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>. The NKVD worked to central quotas. Before, during and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, Stalin also deported whole peoples, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-germans">Volga Germans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>. It is estimated that nearly 3.3 million people were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> between 1941 and 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After Stalin's death on 5 March 1953 and the Khrushchev Thaw, a period of de-Stalinization began in the 1950s and 1960s. Historians disagree about the system's causes and effects. Some, such as Robert Service, blame collectivisation for famines such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a>, while others disagree. Several international legislatures have called the campaign against the kulaks a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, which some historians dispute.</p><p>Rival currents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> judged Stalin differently. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> declared that he was 70 per cent good and 30 per cent bad, and Maoists criticised his view that class contradictions ended once socialism was built. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> kept his own brand of Stalinism, Hoxhaism, for decades after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a>, which isolated the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stalinism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…100 million victims is the estimate the Victims of Communism Memorial Foundation most commonly uses, though some of its own authors distanced themselves from it, helped legitimise comparisons of Nazism and <b>Stalinism</b>, and museums and monuments to the victims stand in Eastern Europe, Canada and the United States.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1934 Karl Radek proposed the name Marxism–Leninism–<b>Stalinism</b> in Pravda, but the name did not catch on, since Stalin and the party's ideologists preferred to keep Marxism–Leninism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Left communists such as Paul Mattick held that the October Revolution was totalitarian from the start, so that Trotskyism differs little from <b>Stalinism</b>; the Trotskyist historian Pierre Broué rejected this.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/trotskyism">Trotskyism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1961 the writer Ralph Giordano, who had followed <b>Stalinism</b> until about 1955, used the phrase as the title of a book settling accounts with it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lied-der-partei">Lied der Partei</a></li><li><blockquote>Mátyás Rákosi, leader of the Hungarian Working People's Party, ruled as a <b>Stalinist</b> dictator, with show trials such as that of László Rajk and a feared secret police, the ÁVH.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-peoples-republic">Hungarian People's Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The regime was strictly <b>Stalinist</b>: land was collectivised, industry nationalised and the Sigurimi secret police watched everyone.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-socialist-republic-of-albania">People's Socialist Republic of Albania</a></li></ul></section>
